WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:08.220
YouTube is the internet’s second largest
search engine and has over one billion active

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.460
monthly users.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:15.660
Over 100 hours of video are uploaded to YouTube
every minute and more content is uploaded

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:24.420
in 60 days than all 3 major U.S. TV Networks
generated in the last 60 years.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.440
It’s fair to say YouTube is a pretty big
deal, here’s some mind bending facts about

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:35.120
this internet giant.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.050
1.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:44.940
The payment processor website PayPal had a
huge impact on YouTubes success.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.930
YouTube was initially funded by the bonuses
received following the eBay buyout of PayPal.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:56.129
If it weren’t for PayPal, YouTube may very
well not exist.

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:57.129
2.

00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:02.989
YouTube founders Chad Hurley, Steve Chen and
Jawed Karim, say two major events inspired

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:04.850
them to create YouTube.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:11.230
Firstly Karim had trouble finding online footage
of Janet Jackson’s Super Bowl wardrobe malfunction.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:17.200
And later, Chen and Hurley had difficulty
sharing a video shot at a dinner party in

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.430
San Francisco in 2005.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:20.500
3.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.830
YouTube actually started out life as a dating
website.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:29.490
It began as a video dating site called “Tune
In Hook Up”, inspired by the site Hot or

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.640
Not.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.150
Users were allowed to rate the attractiveness
of potential partners based on their uploaded

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.150
videos.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.760
But the idea didn’t catch on.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.760
4.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:46.830
YouTube caused major problems for the Universal
Tube &amp; Rollform Equipment company based in

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:47.840
Ohio.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:54.680
The company’s domain was utube.com, upon
the launch of the video sharing website, utube.com

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.730
was overwhelmed with visitors attempting to
spell the video sharing website’s address

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.000
phonetically.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.310
Utube.com actually sued the video sharing
website, claiming their business was hurt.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:13.250
but the case failed and utube changed its
website address to utubeonline.com

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.250
5.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:20.360
In 2007 YouTube used as much bandwidth as
the whole Internet did in 2000.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.360
6.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:29.720
In 2006 shortly before Google purchased YouTube
for $1.65 billion, YouTube was losing money

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:36.360
at a rate of $500,000 a month, despite it’s
immense popularity.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.020
Google however turned that around when they
saw its huge advertising potential.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.020
7.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:47.830
During it’s early days, in an attempt to
increase popularity, YouTube offered $100

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.210
to attractive girls who posted 10 or more
videos on their site.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:57.900
The ad was posted on Craigslist, but surprisingly
the YouTube founders didn’t receive a single

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:58.900
response.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:02:59.900
8.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.800
The first ever video uploaded to YouTube was
called “Me at the Zoo” and shows YouTube

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.470
cofounder, Jawed Karim, talking about the
long trunks of elephants.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:13.320
It has since got over 16 million views.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.820
The very first words uttered on YouTube were
“All right, so here we are in front of the

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:19.820
elephants.”

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:20.820
9.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:27.080
Just 30% of videos on YouTube make up 99%
of views on the site.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:34.880
Only 0.33% of videos have over 1 million views,
on the other hand, 70% of videos have less

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.530
than 500 views.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:37.530
10.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.680
YouTube has quite an impressive reach globally.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.880
More than 80% of YouTube traffic comes from
outside the US.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:51.110
And YouTube is localised in 61 countries and
across 61 languages.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:52.110
11.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:59.490
Every second there are an average of 46,296
YouTube videos being viewed all around the

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.490
world.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:01.490
12.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.720
YouTube has over 800 million unique monthly
visitors.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.060
That’s more than the entire population of
Europe.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.060
13.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:14.240
YouTube has empowered freedom of speech in
many countries, but it has also been blocked

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.720
in four countries, China, Iran, Pakistan and
Turkmenistan.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:20.720
14.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:34.090
And finally, YouTube’s running cost is estimated
to be around $6 million a month.

